WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.765
Welcome to Module 1 of
Global Secure Access as the

2
00:00:03.765 --> 00:00:07.662
Identity-Aware Network:
decide, design and log first.

3
00:00:07.662 --> 00:00:13.115
Global Secure Access is Microsoft's security
service edge, Entra Internet Access

4
00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:18.087
and Entra Private Access, used as the
agency's identity-aware network layer.

5
00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:22.011
This path teaches the twelve
steps of the Global Secure Access

6
00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:26.312
implementation guide in six modules,
in the order the guide runs them.

7
00:00:26.312 --> 00:00:30.368
This first module covers Step
1, the decision and licensing,

8
00:00:30.368 --> 00:00:34.837
Step 2, the reference architecture,
and the logging half of Step 12.

9
00:00:34.837 --> 00:00:39.440
It takes about forty-five minutes,
plus a lab in your own test tenant.

10
00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.228
Why start here?

11
00:00:44.228 --> 00:00:47.677
Global Secure Access is
three things under one name.

12
00:00:47.677 --> 00:00:52.714
The Microsoft traffic profile comes
with Entra I D P one or P two.

13
00:00:52.714 --> 00:00:56.448
Internet Access and Private
Access are licensed separately,

14
00:00:56.448 --> 00:00:58.790
or come with the Microsoft Entra Suite.

15
00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:03.640
Each has its own gate, so the decision is
made per function, not for the product.

16
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.818
And because nothing should block before
there is a record of what it blocked,

17
00:01:07.818 --> 00:01:10.802
the network logs go on before
the first pilot user does.

18
00:01:10.802 --> 00:01:14.716
Every later Validate step in
this path reads those logs.

19
00:01:14.716 --> 00:01:19.335
So in this module you decide each function,
write the architecture down for your

20
00:01:19.335 --> 00:01:24.170
tenant, take the first hashed configuration
export, and turn on the telemetry.

21
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.833
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

22
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:34.457
You'll explain why the guide runs
its steps out of numeric order.

23
00:01:34.457 --> 00:01:37.178
You'll decide each
function on its own gate,

24
00:01:37.178 --> 00:01:41.995
and record the decision with a dated
availability quote and exit criteria.

25
00:01:41.995 --> 00:01:46.758
You'll name the reference architecture's
seven zones and planes, and who owns each.

26
00:01:46.758 --> 00:01:50.239
You'll export the configuration
as hashed evidence,

27
00:01:50.239 --> 00:01:53.720
and say why the beta network
access A P I is run by hand.

28
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.207
And you'll turn on the four network log
categories and check that each is fresh.

29
00:01:58.207 --> 00:02:02.824
Before you start, read Network and
Transport, chapter five, for the gates,

30
00:02:02.824 --> 00:02:08.145
and skim Security Operations and Telemetry,
chapter three, for the log categories.

31
00:02:08.145 --> 00:02:11.660
The lab runs in a test
tenant, never production.

32
00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.885
Unit 1 is the order.

33
00:02:16.885 --> 00:02:21.689
The guide's steps are numbered to match
the twelve network topics of the Zero Trust

34
00:02:21.689 --> 00:02:25.504
closure update, but they run in a
different order, so the logs exist

35
00:02:25.504 --> 00:02:29.672
before anything is enforced, and
nothing blocks before its path exists.

36
00:02:29.672 --> 00:02:33.497
First the decision, the architecture,
and Step twelve's logging.

37
00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:38.710
Then the client, Microsoft traffic, the
compliant network, and tenant restrictions.

38
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:42.213
Then private applications,
privileged protocols,

39
00:02:42.213 --> 00:02:44.798
internet traffic, and T L S inspection.

40
00:02:44.798 --> 00:02:50.360
Last, coexistence and migration, and Step
twelve again, for the evidence package.

41
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.072
This path follows that order.

42
00:02:52.072 --> 00:02:57.647
One more rule: Microsoft's network access
A P I's are on the Graph beta endpoint.

43
00:02:57.647 --> 00:03:00.433
Learn says beta A P I's
are subject to change

44
00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:03.536
and not supported in
production applications.

45
00:03:03.536 --> 00:03:06.402
So the guide runs
Learn's requests by hand,

46
00:03:06.402 --> 00:03:10.197
as an administrator, and
keeps each result as evidence.

47
00:03:10.197 --> 00:03:13.570
Every lab uses the
guide's placeholder table.

48
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.170
Unit 2 is the decision, one row per
function, because the gates differ.

49
00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:27.389
The Microsoft traffic profile is included
in P one or P two, and with it come source

50
00:03:27.389 --> 00:03:32.608
I P restoration, the compliant network
check, and universal tenant restrictions.

51
00:03:32.608 --> 00:03:36.949
Internet Access needs its own
license or the Microsoft Entra Suite,

52
00:03:36.949 --> 00:03:41.833
and carries web content filtering, threat
intelligence, and T L S inspection.

53
00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:47.236
Private Access is the same: its own
license or the Suite, for Quick Access,

54
00:03:47.236 --> 00:03:51.907
per-app access, private D N S,
and the domain controller sensors.

55
00:03:51.907 --> 00:03:56.139
Branches use remote networks,
which need at least fifty P one and

56
00:03:56.139 --> 00:04:00.371
Internet Access licenses combined,
and carry no Private Access.

57
00:04:00.371 --> 00:04:04.420
For each row, the decision
record says adopt,

58
00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.695
retain the current V P N or
S S E, coexist, or defer.

59
00:04:08.695 --> 00:04:12.618
And it carries a dated availability
quote, the license held,

60
00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:16.590
compensating controls, exit
criteria, and an approver.

61
00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.652
Where are agencies?

62
00:04:21.652 --> 00:04:25.693
Learn's known limitations page
covers the whole product:

63
00:04:25.693 --> 00:04:28.616
Global Secure Access
is available in G C C.

64
00:04:28.616 --> 00:04:32.508
No feature page read for the
guide narrows or widens that,

65
00:04:32.508 --> 00:04:35.490
so the G C C column rests
on that one sentence.

66
00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:40.827
A preview feature, such as the cloud
firewall for clients or the Explicit Forward

67
00:04:40.827 --> 00:04:44.412
Proxy, has a G C C availability
that is not yet specified,

68
00:04:44.412 --> 00:04:47.678
and the guide adopts one
only by a decision record.

69
00:04:47.678 --> 00:04:50.847
The table is as of
November eleventh, 2026,

70
00:04:50.847 --> 00:04:54.577
so re-read the limitations
page before each ring.

71
00:04:54.577 --> 00:05:00.021
And where a row of the decision record
says retain the current service, or defer,

72
00:05:00.021 --> 00:05:05.632
it names its compensating controls: for
example, the current fed-ramp authorized

73
00:05:05.632 --> 00:05:11.350
S S E, per-application V P N access lists,
and named-location Conditional Access.

74
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.875
Unit 3 is the reference architecture,
the first of the guide's six artifacts.

75
00:05:19.875 --> 00:05:21.950
It has seven zones and planes.

76
00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:27.300
Managed endpoints carry the Windows client,
Intune policy and the device identity.

77
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.912
Branches send traffic over an I P sec
tunnel, with no user identity on it.

78
00:05:32.912 --> 00:05:37.863
The control plane, Conditional Access,
decides, and never carries traffic.

79
00:05:37.863 --> 00:05:42.018
The policy plane holds the
forwarding and security profiles,

80
00:05:42.018 --> 00:05:44.137
the T L S policies and signaling.

81
00:05:44.137 --> 00:05:46.742
The data plane is
Microsoft's service edge in

82
00:05:46.742 --> 00:05:49.699
commercial Azure regions,
and the provider owns it.

83
00:05:49.699 --> 00:05:54.242
The agency's private network holds
connector groups, private D N S,

84
00:05:54.242 --> 00:05:58.700
applications and domain controller
sensors, all outbound only.

85
00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:03.025
And the evidence zone is the workspace,
Sentinel and the evidence store.

86
00:06:03.025 --> 00:06:07.346
Step 2 makes it the agency's: which
profiles each population gets,

87
00:06:07.346 --> 00:06:12.889
how many connector groups and where, with
two connectors minimum each, the private

88
00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:18.370
D N S suffixes, which branches use remote
networks, and where telemetry lands.

89
00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.845
Unit 4 is the logs, before the pilot.

90
00:06:24.845 --> 00:06:30.016
Learn says the service keeps traffic and
remote network health logs for thirty days;

91
00:06:30.016 --> 00:06:33.783
longer retention needs export
through diagnostic settings.

92
00:06:33.783 --> 00:06:38.979
So a dedicated Entra diagnostic setting
sends four categories to the workspace:

93
00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:42.080
network access traffic
logs, connection events,

94
00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.570
remote network health logs,
and network access alerts.

95
00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:48.645
Sign-in and audit logs
stay on the core setting.

96
00:06:48.645 --> 00:06:53.803
A freshness query runs daily, and a
sudden drop in traffic volume is an alert

97
00:06:53.803 --> 00:06:58.795
in itself, because it covers offboarding,
a disabled profile, or an outage.

98
00:06:58.795 --> 00:07:00.283
And there's a federal reason.

99
00:07:00.283 --> 00:07:04.787
CISA's Remote User Use Case says that
as users connect more directly to

100
00:07:04.787 --> 00:07:10.132
external services, CISA's visibility must
be preserved through telemetry sharing.

101
00:07:10.132 --> 00:07:15.033
Moving users off a full-tunnel V P N
moves their telemetry to Microsoft,

102
00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:18.583
so agree how it reaches
CISA with the agency's tick

103
00:07:18.583 --> 00:07:22.150
delegates before the V P
N's web path is retired.

104
00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:29.079
The lab runs in a test
tenant, never production,

105
00:07:29.079 --> 00:07:32.175
and the authors haven't
run it, as the page says.

106
00:07:32.175 --> 00:07:36.212
There's no fixture lab in this path,
because the guide has no kit yet.

107
00:07:36.212 --> 00:07:39.838
You need a test tenant with
Global Secure Access onboarded,

108
00:07:39.838 --> 00:07:44.500
an administrator with the Global Secure
Access Administrator or Security

109
00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:49.087
Administrator role activated through
pim, Graph PowerShell, and Azure C L I.

110
00:07:49.087 --> 00:07:52.713
Steps one and two make a lab
folder, connect with read scopes,

111
00:07:52.713 --> 00:07:56.338
and export the licenses and
the tenant's onboarding status.

112
00:07:56.338 --> 00:08:00.400
Compare the licenses by product name
with Learn's licensing section.

113
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.274
Step three takes the baseline
export: nine Graph requests,

114
00:08:05.274 --> 00:08:10.250
each written to a file, then a S
H A two fifty-six hash manifest.

115
00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:14.600
The slide abbreviates; the module
page has every command in full.

116
00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:19.251
The guide's Validate: the tenant shows
onboarded, the license count covers

117
00:08:19.251 --> 00:08:24.180
the pilot and the first ring, and the
export folder and its manifest exist.

118
00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:32.693
Step four turns on the telemetry, in
Bash, as a Security Administrator.

119
00:08:32.693 --> 00:08:37.536
The guide's command puts a diagnostic
setting named G S A network telemetry on

120
00:08:37.536 --> 00:08:42.380
the tenant, pointing at your test workspace,
with the four categories enabled.

121
00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:46.992
This is the lab's first write to
the tenant, and Module 6 removes it.

122
00:08:46.992 --> 00:08:49.593
The GET that follows
should list the setting,

123
00:08:49.593 --> 00:08:52.193
with the four categories
and your workspace.

124
00:08:52.193 --> 00:08:54.029
Step five checks freshness.

125
00:08:54.029 --> 00:08:58.968
In the workspace's Logs, run the guide's
union query across the four tables.

126
00:08:58.968 --> 00:09:04.017
The guide's Validate is that each category
has rows within an hour of pilot traffic.

127
00:09:04.017 --> 00:09:08.143
There's no pilot traffic yet,
so expect few or no rows now.

128
00:09:08.143 --> 00:09:13.520
Run the query again after Module 2's
lab, and keep both results as evidence.

129
00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:19.881
The federal requirement, from NIST special

130
00:09:19.881 --> 00:09:23.145
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

131
00:09:23.145 --> 00:09:28.948
The decision supports P L two, S A nine
and C A three: each function decided and

132
00:09:28.948 --> 00:09:35.407
approved, with its availability, license,
compensating controls and exit criteria.

133
00:09:35.407 --> 00:09:39.258
A decision record isn't mapped
to a key security indicator.

134
00:09:39.258 --> 00:09:45.073
The architecture supports S C seven, A C
four, C M two and C M eight: boundaries,

135
00:09:45.073 --> 00:09:48.410
planes and flows documented and exported,

136
00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:52.033
lined up with two fed-ramp
twenty X indicators.

137
00:09:52.033 --> 00:09:56.650
And the telemetry supports A U two,
A U six, A U twelve and S I four:

138
00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:02.745
the network logs exported and fresh,
lined up with three monitoring indicators.

139
00:10:02.745 --> 00:10:08.069
The evidence supports the controls; your
organization still sets their parameters

140
00:10:08.069 --> 00:10:12.910
and owns the policy, and the assessor
decides whether each is satisfied.

141
00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:18.635
Check yourself with two questions.

142
00:10:18.635 --> 00:10:21.260
First: a preview feature is in your plan.

143
00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.747
What is its G C C availability
in the guide's table?

144
00:10:24.747 --> 00:10:29.068
The answer is b: not yet specified,
because the G C C column rests on

145
00:10:29.068 --> 00:10:33.797
Learn's product-wide sentence, and
that doesn't cover preview features.

146
00:10:33.797 --> 00:10:37.960
Second: why does the guide run Step
twelve's logging before Step three?

147
00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:42.198
The answer is b: so the logs
exist before anything is enforced,

148
00:10:42.198 --> 00:10:45.673
and every later Validate
step has a record to read.

149
00:10:45.673 --> 00:10:50.940
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

150
00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.515
To sum up.

151
00:10:55.515 --> 00:10:57.907
You decided each function on its own gate,

152
00:10:57.907 --> 00:11:02.690
wrote the architecture down for your
tenant, took the first hashed configuration

153
00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:06.840
export, and turned on the network
telemetry before anyone was in a pilot.

154
00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.565
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

155
00:11:09.565 --> 00:11:13.878
the decision record with its dated
availability quote and approver,

156
00:11:13.878 --> 00:11:18.571
the license and tenant status exports,
the architecture record, the export

157
00:11:18.571 --> 00:11:23.490
folder with its manifest, the diagnostic
setting GET, and the freshness result.

158
00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:28.498
Next is Module 2, the client and Microsoft
traffic, where you package the client,

159
00:11:28.498 --> 00:11:30.733
count the devices the service sees,

160
00:11:30.733 --> 00:11:34.340
and enable the Microsoft
traffic profile for the pilot.
